\section*{Localization in Two Dimensions}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. At every positive uniform iid disorder strength, the two-dimensional Anderson operator is claimed to have pure-point spectral type throughout its spectrum.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Scattering matrices are compared through absorption, port closing, and Schur complements. Dimension-independent analytic and determinant-phase estimates control repeated finite-volume propagation, yielding localization of the spectral measure in the infinite square-lattice limit.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
For every positive disorder width in the nearest-neighbor two-dimensional Anderson model with iid uniform potentials, the main theorem proves pure-point spectral type, an orthonormal eigenbasis, and dense eigenvalues throughout the full spectrum. Centering a uniform interval changes only the energy origin, exactly covering Simon Problem 2 as defined in the manuscript model and problem statement. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 261, the selected planar Anderson statement constructs the self-adjoint operator and gives its almost-sure spectral interval. Spectral type, including the claimed pure-point conclusion, is not contained in it. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{PlanarAndersonSpectrum.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

